  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMorning water vapor imagery shows an upper level low near the Pacific\nNW coast with a trough extending south well off the coast of\ncentral Baja. The base of this trough is tapping some tropical\nmoisture with PW in excess of 2\" currently observed on satellite.\nThe bulk of this moisture is forecast to remain to our south, but\na weak and brief Atmospheric River is poised to move into far\nSouthern CA this evening into Sunday morning. Ensemble mean IVT\nfrom the GEFS and EPS are on the order of 300-400 kg/m/s across\nSan Diego County, and closer to 250 kg/m/s further north. A few\nlight showers are possible this morning ahead of the trough, but\nthe bulk of the precipitation from this system is expected to\noccur late this evening into Sunday morning. The majority of the\nhigh-res guidance is holding off on the heavier/more widespread\nprecipitation until after 8 PM, so most will likely luck out on\ndry candy collecting conditions this evening. For the most part\nrainfall rates will be around a third-inch per hour or less, with\nisolated rates of half-inch per hour or more mainly in San Diego \nCounty. Showers will become more scattered and lighter by mid-\nmorning Sunday, then tapering off from north to south Sunday\nafternoon, ending by Sunday evening. \n\nCurrent rainfall forecast is for around 0.35-0.70\" for the coastal\nareas and valleys, 0.50-1\" in the mountains, and around 0.10-0.30\"\nin the deserts, with the highest accumulations in San Diego\nCounty. Snow levels will be over 10,000 ft during the heaviest\nprecipitation, falling to around 9,000 ft as precipitation tapers\noff this evening, leaving only trace amounts on the highest peaks\nof the San Bernardino and San Jacinto Mountains. \n\nTransitory ridging and weak offshore flow builds in for Monday \nand Tuesday for relatively warmer weather, though highs will be a \nfew degrees below normal inland - generally low to mid 70s for the\ncoasts and valleys, low 50s to low 60s in the mountains, upper \n60s in the high desert, and mid 70s to low 80s in the low desert.\nAnother trough is forecast to move inland across the West Coast\ntowards the middle of the week, though there is a lot of spread in\nthe ensembles on the timing of this trough. For now forecast\nremains dry with this system with just some minor cooling. Weak\ntroughing remains in place through Friday with little change in\nhigh temperatures each day.",
